  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nHigh temperatures Saturday afternoon were a single degree shy of a \n1913 record at both Fort Wayne and South Bend amid an unseasonable \n500-mb ridge over the eastern CONUS. We'll cling to one more mild \nNovember day as the upper-air pattern remains amplified ahead of an \napproaching cold front. Filtered sunshine to begin the day followed \nby increasing clouds and rain arriving from east to east by the \nearly afternoon. Somewhat breezy as well with southwest winds of 20-\n25 mph. Rain amounts remain modest at 0.10\" or less owing to the \nlate-day timing and upper-level forcing displaced well to the \nnorthwest. Cool northwest flow sets in by the early evening hours \npropelling Sunday night lows toward freezing (clouds may keep \nnorthwest OH in the upper-30s). \n\nVery brief and inconsequential lake response from a secondary surge \nof cold air advection Monday night. Fetch and incoming high pressure \nlimit the overall moister depth, but increasing concensus yields \na narrow area (in both time and space) for 20% POPs. \n\nFor late-week, the ECMWF has joined the GFS in what strikes me as an \nunusual evolution of dual upper-level lows emerging over the central \nand southern US. Concensus revels a deep trough in the lee of the \nRockies, extending through far southern Texas. Encountering a \nsubtropical ridge off the Florida coast this energy is shunted \npoleward along and east of the Mississippi River. The result is \nstrong moisture transport and lackluster cold air given the \ncompeting air masses. POPs have increased locally to a 40-50% chance \nWednesday evening through Tuesday with precipitation types generally \nfollowing a diurnal curve (rain/snow overnight, changing to rain \nduring the day).  The primary trough responsible for this system \narrives to the west Coast tonight, improving model-guidance sampling \nin the near future.\n\nIn the wake of this feature, troughing favors the Midwest through \nthe Thanksgiving weekend while zonal flow resumes for the start of \nDecember.",
